      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Most of my career has been spent inside large, regulated organisations —
        aviation, financial services, consultancy — designing and securing
        infrastructure where downtime and data loss carry real consequences. That
        means cloud migrations that had to work first time, security architecture
        that had to satisfy auditors, and automation that had to be maintainable
        by whoever came next.
      </p>

      <h2>Why independent work</h2>
      <p>
        Large programmes teach discipline, but smaller organisations often need
        the same standard of thinking without a consultancy's overhead. A small
        business moving to the cloud faces the same identity, backup and
        segmentation questions as a large one — the answers are just
        proportionate. That gap is the interesting part.
      </p>

      <h2>On AI</h2>
      <p>
        I use AI tooling daily in delivery and I am genuinely enthusiastic about
        it, which is exactly why I try to be careful about it. Agents wired into
        real systems are privileged accounts, and most of the value comes from
        infrastructure and security judgement rather than clever prompting. If AI
        will not help with your problem, I will say so.
      </p>

      <h2>How engagements usually run</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>A conversation first, at no cost, to work out whether the problem and I fit</li>
        <li>A written view of scope, approach and risks before anyone commits</li>
        <li>Fixed price where the work can be defined; day rate where it genuinely cannot</li>
        <li>Documentation and handover treated as part of delivery, not an extra</li>
      </ul>

